<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Melayu Islam Beraja</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1984">1984</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Malay Islamic Monarchy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Melayu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> Beraja, or Malay Islamic Monarchy, is the national ideology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a>, proclaimed at independence in 1984. It presents the state as a Malay nation guided by Islamic values under the rule of the sultan, combining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-language">Malay language</a> and culture, Islam of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i school</a> and monarchy. It is taught in schools and used to justify the absolute authority of the sultan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/melayu-islam-beraja.jpg" alt="Melayu Islam Beraja" width="320" height="281" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: extracted from the flag of Brunei, provided at the Open Clip Art website. · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_Brunei.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The idea goes back to the Kesultanan Melayu Islam, the Islamic Malay Sultanate, a term first used in 1957 and supported by Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omar-ali-saifuddien-iii">Omar Ali Saifuddien III</a> on the initial recommendations of the Jawatankuasa Tujuh Serangkai. The declaration of 1984 rested on discussions held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 30 September 1957. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> and the United Kingdom signed their agreement of 1979 on five years of preparation for independence, the Information Department of Brunei spread the concept, and it was named Melayu Islam Beraja in the Declaration of Independence proclaimed by Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassanal-bolkiah">Hassanal Bolkiah</a> on 1 January 1984.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>It is described as &quot;a blend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-language">Malay language</a>, culture, and Malay customs, the teaching of Islamic laws and values and the monarchy system which must be esteemed and practiced by all.&quot; Its Malay element presents <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> as a Malay nation that keeps traditional values and culture handed down through the generations, with the Malay language used in all official business and as a shared language between the country's groups. The seven indigenous peoples named in the 1959 Brunei Constitution, the Brunei Malay, Kedayan, Belait Malay, Tutong Malay, Bisaya, Dusun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murut">Murut</a>, are counted together as Brunei Malay.</p><p>Its Islamic element draws on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">Hadith</a>. The 1959 Constitution made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> the official religion of the state, which it had long been in practice as the guiding principle of the sultanate's administration. The monarchy element places the sultan at the head of the royal institution and of the state, with the authority that the 1959 Constitution set out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The concept is presented as the foundation of national life and of the conduct of government in all matters. The sultan has called on citizens to practise and carry out the teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as part of it, and Radio Television Brunei, the state broadcaster and the country's main media outlet, planned to put the idea into practice.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Melayu Islam Beraja” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Since independence Brunei has built its national identity on the ideology of <b>Melayu Islam Beraja</b>, Malay Islamic Monarchy, which presents the sultanate as the continuation of a monarchy that has ruled for more than six centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
